Mild Rheumatoid Arthritis Seropositive
Complex Fatigue Entities
Early-stage symmetrical polyarthritis

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-034
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of intractable joint pain, acute feverishness, and severe immobility to rule out acute sepsis or joint infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 24-hour escalation of total body stiffness, severe pain in the small joints of the hands and feet, and inability to ambulate safely. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was critical to exclude acute systemic infection or septic arthritis, and to provide potent pharmacological relief to manage severe distress before regular general practice hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My joints are completely locked, I feel feverish and exhausted, and the pain is driving me to tears.
History of Present Illness: Patient with recently diagnosed seropositive Rheumatoid Arthritis reports a severe acute exacerbation of early-stage symmetrical polyarthritis. The flare evolved rapidly over 24 hours following a mild viral illness. Symptoms include severe morning stiffness lasting greater than 3 hours, visible MCP and PIP joint swelling, and debilitating systemic fatigue. Patient reports their feet feel like they are "walking on glass" due to metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joint involvement.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly dependent on spouse for all mobility and transfers.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high fall risk due to MTP joint pain and generalized stiffness. Spouse is highly anxious.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 mmHg | HR: 98 bpm | Temp: 37.6 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 76 Kg
General: Looks unwell, fatigued, avoiding any active movement of all four limbs.
Physical Exam: Mild low-grade fever noted. Symmetrical, boggy, tender swelling (synovitis) present across bilateral MCP, PIP, and MTP joints. Wrists are warm and restricted in range of motion. Squeeze test across MCP and MTP joints is exquisitely positive bilaterally. No focal, unilateral hot joint suggestive of sepsis. Findings perfectly match an acute flare of early-stage symmetrical polyarthritis with systemic inflammatory signs.
Environmental Observations: Obstacles cleared from bedside by spouse; walking frame positioned nearby.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently on Plaquenil (Hydroxychloroquine). Took OTC paracetamol with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild Rheumatoid Arthritis Seropositive - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Widespread, bilateral, symmetrical involvement rather than a single, severely inflamed, erythematous joint.

Viral Polyarthropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Established seropositive diagnosis and classical distribution (MCP/PIP/MTP) with boggy synovitis.

Fibromyalgia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Objective, observable synovial swelling, warmth, and low-grade fever rather than purely subjective soft-tissue tender points.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute, immobilizing polyarthritis and low-grade fever warranted an immediate home visit to clinically exclude dangerous differential diagnoses such as septic arthritis or systemic sepsis. After confirming an acute, severe autoimmune flare of their established RA, urgent intervention with systemic corticosteroids was necessary. The patient was in a state of severe pain and functional collapse; withholding treatment until the next in-hours period would have likely precipitated a fall or an unnecessary, highly painful ambulance transfer to the emergency department.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short, rapid-tapering course of oral Prednisolone (starting at 25mg daily for 3 days) to forcefully suppress the acute systemic autoimmune flare. Targeted intervention strategy includes Low Dose Doxycycline (anti-collagenase), Omega-3, and LDN.
Positive Behavioral Change: By engaging in 5 minutes of gentle, warm-water hand and foot soaks every morning, you will actively dilate the blood vessels and reduce joint stiffness, directly rewarding yourself with improved mobility and less pain during your first steps of the day.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Recommended strictly wearing cushioned, supportive footwear indoors to protect the MTP joints.
Follow-up: Urgent GP and Rheumatologist review in 5 days to assess flare resolution and escalate DMARD therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if a single joint becomes disproportionately swollen, turns bright red and hot, or if fever spikes above 38.5 C with rigors.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Rheumatoid flares require aggressive, short-term management to prevent permanent joint damage, but long-term care relies on optimizing your specialist medications.
Clinical Course: The oral steroids will dramatically reduce the joint swelling, stiffness, and low-grade fever within 24-48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you develop uncontrollable shivering, a high fever, or if one specific joint suddenly becomes fiery red.

